Apply the first relation to an eigenstate of $L_z$: $\begin{aligned}L_zL_+\ket{m}
&=\left(L_+L_z+[L_z,L_+]\right)\ket{m}\\
&=(m+1)\hbar\,L_+\ket{m}.\end{aligned}$ Unless $L_+\ket{m}=0$, it is another eigenstate of $L_z$, now with eigenvalue $(m+1)\hbar$. Similarly, $L_zL_-\ket{m}
=(m-1)\hbar\,L_-\ket{m}.$ Thus $L_+$ raises $m$ by one and $L_-$ lowers it by one.

